# Migrating `BackgroundProcessor` to .NET 8

## Key Breaking Changes

1. **`Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException`** — removed entirely in .NET 5+
2. **`Thread.ResetAbort()` is also gone** — no equivalent needed
3. **`ThreadAbortException` will never be raised** — catch block is dead code

## The Modern Replacement

Use `CancellationToken` + cooperative cancellation, ideally with `BackgroundService` or a `Task`-based pattern:

```csharp
public class BackgroundProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private Task? _workerTask;
    private CancellationTokenSource? _cts;

    public void Start()
    {
        _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
        _workerTask = Task.Run(() => ProcessLoop(_cts.Token));
    }

    private async Task ProcessLoop(CancellationToken token)
    {
        while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            try
            {
                // Pass token so blocking calls become cancellable
                var item = await GetNextItemAsync(token);
                await ProcessItemAsync(item, token);
            }
            catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
            {
                // Graceful shutdown requested — roll back partial work
                RollbackCurrentTransaction();
                break;
            }
            finally
            {
                FlushBuffers();
            }
        }
    }

    public async Task StopAsync(TimeSpan? timeout = null)
    {
        if (_cts is null || _workerTask is null)
            return;

        _cts.Cancel();

        // Wait for graceful exit with a timeout
        var completed = await Task.WhenAny(
            _workerTask,
            Task.Delay(timeout ?? TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5)));

        if (completed != _workerTask)
        {
            // Worker didn't stop in time — log a warning.
            // There is NO forced-kill equivalent in .NET 8.
        }
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        _cts?.Cancel();
        _cts?.Dispose();
    }
}
```

## If You Must Keep Synchronous Blocking

Replace `Thread.Abort()` with `Thread.Interrupt()` (still works in .NET 8 — unblocks `Thread.Sleep`, `Monitor.Wait`, etc.):

```csharp
public void Stop()
{
    _running = false;
    if (_workerThread is { IsAlive: true })
    {
        // Interrupt unblocks the thread if it's in a wait state
        _workerThread.Interrupt();
        if (!_workerThread.Join(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5)))
        {
            // Thread is truly stuck — log and move on.
            // There is no safe forced termination in .NET 8.
        }
    }
}
```

Catch `ThreadInterruptedException` instead of `ThreadAbortException`.

## Common Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Guidance |
|---------|----------|
| **Blocking `GetNextItem()` ignores cancellation** | Refactor to accept `CancellationToken` or use an async collection like `Channel<T>` |
| **`Thread.Sleep` in Stop()** | Replace with `Thread.Join(timeout)` or `Task.WhenAny` — sleeping is a race condition |
| **No forced kill exists** | Design all loops to check `CancellationToken` frequently; there's no safety net |
| **Fire-and-forget `Task.Run`** | Always store the `Task` and observe exceptions to avoid silent failures |
| **`volatile bool` for signaling** | Works but `CancellationToken` is the idiomatic .NET 8 mechanism and integrates with async APIs |

## ASP.NET Core Integration (Bonus)

If this runs inside a web host, inherit from `BackgroundService`:

```csharp
public class Processor : BackgroundService
{
    protected override async Task ExecuteAsync(CancellationToken stoppingToken)
    {
        while (!stoppingToken.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            var item = await GetNextItemAsync(stoppingToken);
            await ProcessItemAsync(item, stoppingToken);
        }
    }
}

// Registration in Program.cs:
builder.Services.AddHostedService<Processor>();
```

The host handles start/stop lifecycle and cancellation automatically.
